Experiment: Intrusion Detection System using Snort (Kali Linux)
🎯 Aim

To configure and use Snort as an Intrusion Detection System (IDS) to monitor and analyze network traffic in Kali Linux.

🎯 Objectives
To understand the concept of IDS and IPS
To install and configure Snort
To monitor network traffic using Snort
To analyze packets based on predefined rules
To detect suspicious or malicious activity
🧠 Concept (Short for Viva)

Snort is an open-source network security tool that works as:

IDS → Detects and alerts
IPS → Detects and blocks

It uses:

Packet sniffing
Rule-based detection
⚙️ Tools Used
7
Snort (Snort 3)
Kali Linux
🧾 Requirements
Kali Linux
Snort installed
Active network interface (eth0)
🪜 Procedure (Step-by-Step)
🔹 Step 1: Check Network Interface
ip a

👉 Identify interface (eth0)

🔹 Step 2: Run Snort
sudo snort -c /etc/snort/snort.lua -i eth0 -A alert_fast

👉 Snort starts monitoring traffic

🔹 Step 3: Generate Network Traffic

Open another terminal:

ping 8.8.8.8
🔹 Step 4: Observe Output
Snort displays packet activity
Alerts generated if rules match
👀 Observations
Snort successfully loaded configuration and rules
Network traffic was monitored on eth0 interface
ICMP traffic (ping) was observed
No alerts were generated for normal traffic
✅ Result

Snort was successfully configured and used as an Intrusion Detection System to monitor and analyze network traffic in real time.
